re time for a certain  character's chapter for example  yes it does i mean typically and i've  said this many many times before but  tyrion has been  at least for the first three books  tyrion was the easiest chapter to write  his chapters virtually  wrote themselves when i hit books four  and five  tyrion's chapters became much harder to  write because he was in a very dark  place  and he was wrestling with a lot of  demons  and i really had to try to keep that in  mind at all time  bran was traditionally the hardest one  to write because he's a young character  he was the youngest at a regular cast  and um  the uh it's difficult to write from the  viewpoint of a kid because you you know  what's happening in the scene  but is the kid gonna know what's  happening in the scene is he is he  old enough to understand and you have to  try to put it in his language and  all that can be done but it takes time  it takes effort you can't just kind of  let it flow  so but yes as to how long it takes to  write a chapter well you know  two days um 10 years i've had  both cases extina  she's barrio honey on the cover of fresh  donuts wants to know  when will you give theon greyjoy a break  [Laughter]  well i don't know i don't think in terms  of giving the characters a break  but theon has been through some rough  times mind you but he's also done some  awful things  so maybe it's uh maybe it's karma i find  as a writer sometimes the most stressful  and exciting  opportunity is that you have a story  inside of you  and you have to figure out how you want  to communicate it  how do you come up with your overall  theme and narrative structure for a new  book like ice of  fire and game of thrones  you know i don't i don't think in terms  of overall themes or things like that  the theme emerges from the writing of  the book i think in terms of character  and incident and scenes and one thing  leading to  leading to another and looking at the  world  and and trying to tell the truth 